From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 23:12:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] svc.configd: `TX_SIZE()` sign-extends on an LP64 build,
 crashing multi-value commits

`svc.configd` dies of SIGSEGV committing any transaction that sets a property
to more than one value.  One value is fine; two are fatal.  Via `svccfg
import` that is any manifest holding, for instance, a `<dependency>` with two
`<service_fmri>` children -- `cmd/svc/milestone/restarter.xml` is one -- and
the import fails with

    svccfg: Could not delete svc:/TEMP/system/svc/restarter
	(repository connection broken).

which is ECONNABORTED from the door call: configd took the signal, so its own
error paths never ran.

`TX_SIZE(x)` is `P2ROUNDUP((x), sizeof (uint32_t))`, and `P2ROUNDUP(x, align)`
is `(-(-(x) & -(align)))` (`sys/sysmacros.h`).  Every operand it is handed is
a `uint32_t`, so `-(x)` is evaluated in 32 bits and then *zero*-extended to 64
before being masked against a 64-bit `-(align)`; the closing negation
therefore lands in the upper word:

    TX_SIZE((uint32_t)27) = 0xffffffff0000001c
    TX_SIZE((size_t)27)   = 0x000000000000001c

Six of the eight call sites assign the result back into a `uint32_t`, which
truncates the damage away.  The two in `cmd/svc/configd/object.c` that add it
to a pointer do not:

  o	`tx_process_cmds()`, object.c:324, walking the values of a command:

	    v = (uint32_t *)((caddr_t)str + TX_SIZE(*v));

	`v` ends up exactly 2^32 below where it belongs and the next
	iteration's `'%q'` argument faults in sqlite's `vxprintf()`:

	    client_switcher -> tx_commit -> rc_tx_commit -> object_tx_commit
	      -> tx_process_cmds -> backend_tx_run_update
	      -> sqlite_exec_vprintf -> sqlite_vmprintf -> base_vprintf
	      -> vxprintf

  o	`tx_cmd_value()`, object.c:641, seeking to value `val_no`:

	    bp += sizeof (uint32_t) + TX_SIZE(value_len);

	the returned `*val` is 2^32 low, and `generate_value_list()`
	(rc_node.c:3695) faults reading it while assembling the audit
	record.  `rc_tx_commit()` calls that unconditionally on every
	successful commit, so this one is reached even once the first is
	fixed.

Both loops advance only from their second value onwards, which is exactly why
the number of values decides it.

Not reachable as illumos ships today, because `cmd/svc/configd` is still built
32-bit, where `size_t` is 32 bits and both halves agree.  It bites immediately
on a 64-bit configd.

Fixed in `TX_SIZE()` rather than at the two call sites, so that a third one
cannot reintroduce it.  `P2ROUNDUP()` itself is arguably hazardous for any
caller whose operand is narrower than `align`, but changing it reaches the
whole tree, including the kernel; that is a separate and much larger
conversation.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5 (claude-opus-5)
---
 usr/src/common/svc/repcache_protocol.h | 11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/usr/src/common/svc/repcache_protocol.h b/usr/src/common/svc/repcache_protocol.h
index a334cea34a..15e9bc14e5 100644
--- a/usr/src/common/svc/repcache_protocol.h
+++ b/usr/src/common/svc/repcache_protocol.h
@@ -762,7 +762,16 @@ struct rep_protocol_transaction_cmd {
 #define	REP_PROTOCOL_TRANSACTION_CMD_MIN_SIZE \
 	    REP_PROTOCOL_TRANSACTION_CMD_SIZE(0)
 
-#define	TX_SIZE(x)	P2ROUNDUP((x), sizeof (uint32_t))
+/*
+ * The (size_t) cast is load-bearing on an LP64 build.  P2ROUNDUP(x, align)
+ * is (-(-(x) & -(align))): with a uint32_t x, -(x) is evaluated in 32 bits
+ * and then ZERO-extended before being masked against a 64-bit -(align), so
+ * the closing negation lands in the upper word, and TX_SIZE((uint32_t)27)
+ * comes out as 0xffffffff0000001c rather than 0x1c.  Callers that assign the
+ * result back into a uint32_t truncate the damage away; the two in object.c
+ * that add it to a pointer do not.
+ */
+#define	TX_SIZE(x)	P2ROUNDUP((size_t)(x), sizeof (uint32_t))
 
 struct rep_protocol_transaction_request {
 	enum rep_protocol_requestid rpr_request; /* SETUP, ABORT or TEARDOWN */
